---
import AtlasLayout from '../../layouts/AtlasLayout.astro';
import VersionLabel from '../../components/VersionLabel.astro';
import { AUTHOR_SITE, REPO_URL } from '../../config';
import { atlasUrl } from '../../lib/entries';
---

<AtlasLayout
  title="About"
  description="The AI Evaluation Atlas is an independent research project by Monica Kodwani mapping how AI systems are tested, judged, audited, and monitored."
  currentPath="about/"
>
  <header class="entry-header">
    <p class="kicker">About the project</p>
    <h1>About the Atlas</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="guide-prose" style="margin-top: 1.8rem;">
    <p>
      The AI Evaluation Atlas is an independent research project mapping how
      AI systems are tested, judged, audited, and monitored. It focuses on the
      evidence behind evaluation claims: what an instrument measures, how
      reliable it is, and what decisions its results can reasonably support.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Atlas exists because AI evaluation has outgrown its vocabulary.
      Benchmarks, human studies, AI judges, red teams, audits, standards, and
      observatories all produce “results,” and those results are routinely
      asked to carry claims — about safety, fairness, reliability, and trust —
      that they were never designed to support. The Atlas separates the
      instrument from the inference: each entry states what an evaluation
      measures and, just as prominently, what its results do not establish.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is maintained by <a href={AUTHOR_SITE}>Monica Kodwani</a>, a
      human-centered AI researcher whose doctoral work studies how people
      audit and trust generative AI. The Atlas grew out of, and feeds back
      into, ongoing research on AI evaluation science — including a planned
      systematic review of the trustworthy-AI evaluation literature — and is
      intended as a long-lived companion to that work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Atlas is an evolving research resource, not a finished reference.
      The current release is <VersionLabel /> — it establishes the structure,
      taxonomy, lifecycle map, and first entries, with many entries
      deliberately published as marked drafts while source verification
      proceeds. The <a href={atlasUrl('methodology/')}>methodology</a> explains
      how entries are classified and verified, and the
      <a href={atlasUrl('changelog/')}>changelog</a> records how the Atlas
      changes over time.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is open source and lives in its own
      <a href={REPO_URL}>project repository</a>. Corrections and source
      suggestions are welcome.
    </p>
  </div>
</AtlasLayout>
